1: # The actual reduced-III page and positive parameter return
2: 
3: Status: complete comparison draft submitted for independent audit.
4: This note continues `part3_iii_star_reduced_cap.md`; the construction
5: and integral topology there have a separate reserved statement P3F.37.
6: No new manuscript return statement is assigned before the present
7: maps and orientation comparison are audited.
8: 
9: Fix m=4l+3, l>=0, and the source
10: \[
11:  S:\ y^2=x^3+tx+1,\quad G=(0,1),\quad
12:  M=\mathcal O(2G-O),\quad Q=G.
13: \]
14: Use the single reduced-III cap with A_r^m=B_{r^m}, in the marked
15: single-A cut convention, and choose one of the three I1 fibres as
16: the simple whole-fibre zero. The parameter is r, with lambda=r^m.
17: The same argument below also treats the explicit second source
18: \[
19:  S':\ y^2=x^3+tx-t,\quad G=(1,1),
20: \]
21: whose fibres are III* at infinity, II at zero, and I1 at -27/4.
22: The precise root and source-complement extension for this second
23: source is recorded at the end.
24: 
25: ## III-R.1. The exact positive angular clutch
26: 
27: Let t_c be the selected I1 value, q=t-t_c,
28: s=log(q)/(2pi i), and v=log(r)/(2pi i). The actual multiplier
29: of the suspension line in the chosen cusp frame is
30: exp(2pi i C(q)) q r^m. Thus its periods are
31: \[
32:  \gamma(s,v)=\binom{\Gamma(q)}{C(q)+s+mv},\qquad
33:  \delta=\binom01,
34: \]
35: where Gamma is the holomorphic Tate logarithm of -G and C is a
36: holomorphic unit logarithm. The construction note proves that the
37: positive parameter deck is (v,zeta)->(v+1,zeta-s delta).
38: Its inverse closing map multiplies the line by t-t_c.
39: 
40: Use the auxiliary cusp cover q=Q_0^{2m}. The two extending origins
41: are
42: \[
43:  o(s,v)=\binom{-\Gamma s/m}{-Cs/m-s^2/(2m)-sv},\qquad
44:  \sigma(s,v)=\binom{\Gamma v}{Cv+m(v^2-v)/2}.
45: \]
46: Their exact differences are
47: \[
48:  \begin{aligned}
49:  o(s,v+1)-o(s,v)&=-s\delta,\\
50:  \sigma(s,v+1)-\sigma(s,v)&=-s\delta+\gamma(s,v),\\
51:  o(s+2m,v)-o(s,v)&=-2\gamma(s,v)-2m\delta,\\
52:  \sigma(s+2m,v)-\sigma(s,v)&=0.
53:  \end{aligned}                                                   \tag{III-R.1}
54: \]
55: The factor 2 in this auxiliary cusp cover is retained: m is odd,
56: and the difference for s->s+m would contain -m delta/2.
57: The discrepancies in (III-R.1) are actual integral periods.
58: The cusp origin sigma has bounded characters in q and hence extends
59: in the actual unimodular cusp charts. Subtracting o removes the
60: parameter deck on the ordinary collar. For every nonzero q its
61: remaining multiplier is an invertible unit times r^m; the actual
62: m-node parameter chain, with charts X_iY_i=r and
63: X_i=V/r^i, Y_i=r^{i+1}/V, gives the other extending origin.
64: These are two collar comparisons, with no asserted two-variable
65: proper analytic corner.
66: 
67: Their difference is
68: \[
69:  F(s,v)=\binom{\Gamma(s/m+v)}
70:  {C(s/m+v)+(s+mv)^2/(2m)-mv/2}.
71: \]
72: Direct subtraction gives
73: \[
74:  F(s,v+\theta)-F(s,v)
75:    =\theta\gamma(s,v)+\frac m2\theta(\theta-1)\delta.
76:                                                                \tag{III-R.2}
77: \]
78: The last coefficient is zero at both endpoints. Its exponential is
79: a periodic unitary gauge, homotopic through endpoint-fixed gauges
80: to the constant gauge. It disappears in the delta-circle quotient.
81: Under the retained cusp deck s->s+2m, this angular difference changes
82: by 2m theta delta. Its line transition has winding 2m. Consequently
83: the actual positive r-loop makes one positive gamma turn, with the
84: same sign convention as P3F.23 and P3K.10. It is not an m-fold
85: positive gamma turn.
86: 
87: ## III-R.2. The marked Seifert space and its page
88: 
89: The ordinary quotient by the source elliptic direction has coordinates
90: (t,gamma). At the cap put q_infty=1/t and use the actual root variable
91: z, q_infty=z^m, and compatible positive norm coordinate u. The
92: single-A cut convention is
93: \[
94:  (z,u)\sim(\zeta_m z,u-1),\qquad
95:  u=m\gamma-\arg(q_\infty)/(2\pi).
96: \]
97: This is precisely the weighted circle fibration on the standard
98: three-sphere with weights (1,m). Here is an explicit map retaining
99: the sign and meridian. Let R(z)>0 be the unique solution of
100: \[
101:  R^2+|z|^2R^{2/m}=1.
102: \]
103: Send (z,u) to
104: \[
105:  (z_1,z_2)=
106:  \bigl(zR^{1/m}e^{2\pi i u/m},\ R e^{2\pi i u}\bigr)\in S^3.
107:                                                                \tag{III-R.3}
108: \]
109: The two factors from (z,u)->(zeta_m z,u-1) cancel exactly.
110: The inverse on the cap is obtained by choosing a real argument of
111: z_2 and then recovering z; a different choice is exactly that
112: equivalence. The base coordinate is t=z_2/z_1^m and the positive
113: gamma action is (z_1,z_2)->(e^{2pi i theta}z_1,
114: e^{2pi i m theta}z_2). Thus q maps to -h and gamma to m h,
115: as in the construction's degree-one gluing matrix.
116: 
117: Choose a regular binding at t=t_* close to, but distinct from,
118: t_c. Its positive Milnor function is z_2-t_*z_1^m. The affine
119: page is the graph
120: \[
121:  b-t_*a^m=1,
122: \]
123: embedded in S^3 by (a,b)->(rho a,rho^m b), where rho>0 is
124: the unique solution of rho^2|a|^2+rho^{2m}|b|^2=1.
125: Its inverse divides by the positive m-th root of the Milnor
126: function on the selected argument page. Its projection is exactly
127: \[
128:                          t=t_*+a^{-m}.                \tag{III-R.4}
129: \]
130: The compactification is P1_a. It is branched to order m at a=0
131: (the additive cap) and a=infinity (the regular binding). The open
132: page C^o is a disc, with one boundary circle at the binding. The
133: chosen cusp and every other finite source critical value are
134: unramified. Each has m preimages. This retains the regular-binding
135: cover; a cover branched instead at the cusp is a different map.
136: 
137: ## III-R.3. The actual cap slice and line on the page
138: 
139: The full cap orbit is the actual mapping torus of a_0 on the
140: resolved root source, in the same norm coordinate u. In (III-R.3),
141: the page equation is
142: \[
143:  2\pi u+\arg(1-t_*z^m)=\varphi\pmod{2\pi}.
144: \]
145: Choose the small cap disc so the displayed argument has a single
146: real branch. Its lifts are the graphs
147: \[
148:  u=u_0(z)+j,\quad
149:  u_0(z)=\varphi/(2\pi)-\arg(1-t_*z^m)/(2\pi),\quad j\in Z.
150: \]
151: The actual A_r map sends one graph to the next while applying
152: a_0 to its source point. Every orbit has a unique representative
153: on j=0: the function u_0 is invariant under z->zeta_m z, so
154: two such representatives differing by A_r^j would satisfy
155: u_0(z)=u_0(z)-j, which forces j=0. Hence the map
156: \[
157:  p\longmapsto[p,u_0(z(p))]
158: \]
159: identifies the entire resolved III source tube with the actual
160: page near the exceptional core. It includes both components and
161: their tangency point. There is no residual stabilizer quotient.
162: The principal delta circle above that graph is the norm sphere
163: of N=O(2G'-O) with a prescribed smooth positive radius. Radial
164: normalization identifies it with the unit circle of that exact
165: line N. In particular its component degrees are zero.
166: 
167: On the other finite pieces the page is the unramified pullback of
168: the ordinary source and line. The fixed-cusp orbit product is the
169: actual P3F.9 comparison relative to the source and gamma boundary
170: marking. The page is chosen disjoint from the fixed sphere, as in
171: P3K.6 with degree m; its m phase values avoid the short compression
172: arc. These maps construct a locally translation-isomorphic affine
173: elliptic fibration over C^o, including the resolved III and the
174: actual nodal fibres. Its Jacobian is the minimal source pullback
175: under (III-R.4).
176: 
177: Here is a comparison that also fixes the actual binding collar.
178: Let Lambda be its elliptic period local system on the complement
179: of the critical values. In one sector of (III-R.4), the three
180: original nodal vanishing paths lift with their original marking.
181: Their vanishing cycles span Lambda integrally, by the trace-mod-d^2
182: calculation in the construction note. Each transvection image is
183: the primitive subgroup generated by its vanishing cycle. Thus the
184: global coinvariant group is zero. Poincare duality with compact
185: supports on the punctured oriented base gives
186: H_c^2(Lambda)=H_0(Lambda)=0. The quotient j_*Lambda/j_!Lambda is
187: a finite skyscraper sheaf and has H^1=0. Its exact sequence then
188: gives
189: \[
190:               H^2(C^o,\partial C^o;j_*\Lambda)=0.       \tag{III-R.5}
191: \]
192: For the source with II+I1, the two vanishing cycles in a morsification
193: of the actual II fibre meet once and already span the lattice, so
194: the same calculation applies. That local morsification is used only
195: to compute the two integral vanishing relations of the cusp fibre.
196: 
197: Use the sheaf of smooth sections of the Neron translation group,
198: whose translations extend to the actual minimal regular source.
199: Its identity part has the local exponential sequence with kernel
200: j_*Lambda and a fine Lie-algebra sheaf. Surjectivity at a reduced
201: III fibre follows from its additive identity group and the analytic
202: inverse-function theorem; at a node it is the corresponding
203: multiplicative exponential. Its component quotient is the
204: skyscraper Z/2 at the III value. Its H^1, also relative to the
205: ordinary binding collar, is zero. Consequently (III-R.5) removes
206: the translation cocycle relative to that collar. More explicitly,
207: remove its component cocycle by local component sections, lift its
208: remaining cocycle to the Lie bundle, remove the integral period
209: two-cocycle by (III-R.5), and solve the resulting smooth Lie cocycle
210: with a partition of unity vanishing on the binding collar. The
211: resulting translations are mutually inverse smooth isomorphisms
212: to the sectioned pullback, equal to the given comparison on that
213: collar. Their extensions across III are the extending source
214: translations already used in the cap.
215: 
216: The line comparison retains more than the generic fibre degree.
217: On the cap it is the actual N just identified. Everywhere else
218: its fibrewise Picard point is 2G', as fixed by the two elliptic
219: period multipliers. Translation acts trivially on this Picard
220: point, including the specialization at III. One can see the latter
221: by taking the extending translation on the smooth total source:
222: the equality of degree-zero Picard points holds off the central
223: fibre, and its two multidegrees remain zero, so their vertical
224: divisor difference is in the integral kernel of the reduced III
225: intersection matrix, namely an integral whole fibre. Thus the
226: ratio line is a base pullback. Normalize its trivializing fibre
227: section at the chosen origin; its transition functions are then
228: constant on every compact connected fibre. This gives the actual
229: base line, also through the additive fibre. Its absolute Chern
230: class on C^o vanishes. Keeping the prescribed binding gauge can
231: leave an integral relative base class nF; it has square zero and
232: zero product with M, since M.F=F^2=0. No such gauge is discarded.
233: 
234: The actual circle line is flat on the boundary T3: on a regular
235: elliptic torus its degree-zero multipliers and the commuting
236: translation lift admit a unitary flat representative. It therefore
237: has zero boundary Chern class. P3K.5 supplies the same relative
238: square for every retained boundary gauge. This completes the
239: page, source, and line comparison required by the real trace.
240: 
241: ## III-R.4. Exact characteristic data and the return
242: 
243: The compact sectioned pullback has a III fibre and 3m nodal fibres
244: for S; for S' it has a III fibre, m II fibres, and m I1 fibres.
245: In either case its Euler number is 3+3m and
246: \[
247:  \chi=\frac{m+1}{4}=l+1,\qquad \sigma=-8\chi.
248: \]
249: The pulled section 2G has height 2m. It lies in the identity
250: component of III, and all other fibres are irreducible. Hence
251: the actual height/intersection formula gives
252: \[
253:  P\cdot O=m-\chi=3l+2,\qquad
254:  M^2=(P-O)^2=-2\chi-2P\cdot O=-2m.               \tag{III-R.6}
255: \]
256: The intersections can be located directly. For S the root section
257: G'=(0,z^{3l+3}) has doubling slope 1/(2z^{3l+2}); consequently
258: the zero-section parameter of 2G' is
259: -X(2G')/Y(2G')=2z^{3l+2} times a unit. For S' the slope is
260: (1+3z^{4l+3})/(2z^{3l+2}), with the same leading zero-section
261: parameter. Thus all 3l+2 intersections in (III-R.6) occur at the
262: actual reduced III cap, with the stated multiplicity. No extra
263: intersection is inserted at the regular binding.
264: 
265: The regular cap of the page is T2 times D2, of zero signature.
266: The real compression cap in P3K.6 kills the nodal vanishing cycle
267: instead of the binding cycle; its H2 restriction to the marked T3
268: is injective. The line and determinant restrict trivially on that
269: boundary. The actual graph and compression therefore give exactly
270: the integral Euler-line comparison and square calculation in
271: P3K.6--7, with
272: \[
273:  s_\delta=-2m,\qquad p_{\rm rel}=3\sigma=-24\chi.
274: \]
275: All hypotheses there have now been supplied by maps: the orbit is
276: the proved S5, the action is semifree with one O(-1)^2 fixed curve,
277: the cusp translation is in the smooth identity component, the
278: degree-m page avoids the same short gamma compression arc, and
279: (III-R.2) identifies the physical positive r-loop with one positive
280: gamma turn. The two boundary orientation reversals retained in
281: P3K.7 therefore give
282: \[
283:  A(\Phi)=\frac{12s_\delta-p_{\rm rel}}{24}
284:           =\chi-m=-\frac{3m-1}{4}=-(3l+2).             \tag{III-R.7}
285: \]
286: With the Eells--Kuiper convention fixed in P3F.22, the Hopf transfer
287: has mu=A/28 and oriented sphere class -(3l+2) modulo28.
288: At m=3 this is A=-2 and class26. The mapping-torus conclusion for
289: each nonzero residue is the one proved in P3F.24. These formulas
290: are derived from the actual r-family, rather than assigned to a
291: sectioned cover without its torsor and angular comparison.
292: 
293: ## III-R.5. The explicit second OS43 source
294: 
295: For y^2=x^3+tx-t the discriminant is a nonzero constant times
296: t^2(4t+27). At t=0 the orders of f,g,Delta are (1,1,2), giving
297: II; at -27/4 it is a simple I1. At infinity q=1/t the exact
298: minimal equation is
299: \[
300:  y_0^2=x_0^3+q^3x_0-q^5,\qquad G=(q^2,q^3),
301: \]
302: giving III*. The surface is rational by the explicit inverse
303: t=(y^2-x^3)/(x-1). The good fourth root sends G to the nonzero
304: fixed two-torsion point (0,0), so its III* component is nonzero.
305: The E7 discriminant and the section's disjointness from O give
306: h(G)=1/2 and prove that G is primitive with no Mordell--Weil
307: torsion, exactly as for the first source. In particular 2G.O=0
308: on the original rational surface, and its Hom line for Q=G has
309: degree one and zero component multidegrees. Its sole zero can be
310: the indicated I1.
311: 
312: For m=4l+3, k=3l+2, the actual substitution q=z^m,
313: x_0=z^{2k}X,y_0=z^{3k}Y gives
314: \[
315:  Y^2=X^3+zX-z^{2l+3},\qquad
316:  G'=(z^{2l+2},z^{3l+3}).
317: \]
318: Its quadratic singular part is Y^2-zX. The same one-point
319: resolution gives reduced III, with G' on the nonidentity component
320: and 2G' on the identity component. The deck and its exact m-division,
321: line norm, meridian, and primitive boundary maps are the ones in
322: the construction note. The integral source basis O,F,E7,G has the
323: same unimodular Gram matrix, and its translation on the complement
324: still sends M to M-F. The II fibre contributes the two primitive
325: vanishing cycles with intersection one; thus its source complement
326: is simply connected. This verifies all unchanged integral topology
327: maps for this explicit source, without identifying the two source
328: surfaces or their full singular-fibre maps with one another.
